    "recommendations": "Reducing fuel continuity within the shrub and brush concentrations on the property, particularly where heavy brush and continuous shrub cover exist, could improve defensible space around the structure and reduce opportunities for fire to move through the landscape. Where scattered shrubs transition into denser brush concentrations, increasing separation between vegetation clusters may further reduce fuel continuity and support suppression operations. The single access route serving the property warrants attention, as maintaining vegetation clearance along both sides of the driveway or access road could improve firefighter access and support evacuation. Managing vegetation height and density along the access corridor would help preserve visibility and passability under adverse conditions. The open terrain areas on the property represent a natural fuel break that is worth preserving by limiting encroachment from adjacent brush and mixed vegetation. Continued vegetation management near any structures or improvements at the wildland interface could meaningfully reduce structure exposure and support long-term wildfire resilience."
